1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,520
Hello, fellow aliens.

2
00:00:01,935 --> 00:00:09,082
Today we will learn about seemingly simple words like "Hello", "Please", or "Thank you", and about the complex interactions behind them.

3
00:00:09,324 --> 00:00:11,968
We may call these interactions "Social mechanics".

4
00:00:48,816 --> 00:00:53,828
Galactic protocols require that the first sentence of any first contact must be the sentence "We come in peace".

5
00:00:54,111 --> 00:00:55,361
This is called a greeting.

6
00:00:55,721 --> 00:00:59,861
Of course, this sentence is optional in case you don't actually come in peace.

7
00:01:02,461 --> 00:01:04,528
Earthlings always greet each other when they encounter.

8
00:01:05,237 --> 00:01:07,157
They are not the only race to do so.

9
00:01:08,077 --> 00:01:14,345
The Lava Golems from Sanguis 4 for example greet each other by describing what they will do if ever they meet in mortal combat.

10
00:01:14,829 --> 00:01:23,709
One Golem may say: "I will rip your guts out and wrap them around your head!", to which the other one may reply "I will turn you inside out and cover you with yellow sauce"!

11
00:01:24,069 --> 00:01:29,522
And that's only how they greet the mailman - greetings between close siblings or written greetings are much longer.

12
00:01:29,522 --> 00:01:32,758
For Golems, greetings serve to show off social dominance.

13
00:01:33,446 --> 00:01:35,282
Earthling greetings have another purpose.

14
00:01:35,767 --> 00:01:38,247
It has to do with so-called social relationships.

15
00:01:38,787 --> 00:01:41,347
To understand this, let me digress a bit.

16
00:01:42,167 --> 00:01:43,227
This is a sofa.

17
00:01:43,687 --> 00:01:45,201
Its purpose doesn't matter here.

18
00:01:45,554 --> 00:01:55,714
What matters is that A) Many earthlings have a sofa, B) Earthlings take their sofa with them when they move, and, most importantly, C) You need more than one earthling to move a sofa.

19
00:01:55,714 --> 00:02:03,454
So, earthlings need to cooperate to move all those sofas around, which makes them perfect examples for how earthlings organize cooperation.

20
00:02:07,134 --> 00:02:10,505
The key to cooperation management is social relationships.

21
00:02:10,907 --> 00:02:15,839
Besides family bonds, earthlings create more or less intense bonds with other earthlings.

22
00:02:16,162 --> 00:02:19,322
The intensity of the relationship determines the level of cooperation.

23
00:02:20,922 --> 00:02:23,902
For example, a stranger would not care about your sofa.

24
00:02:24,222 --> 00:02:26,809
An acquaintance would sit on your sofa when you invite him.

25
00:02:27,053 --> 00:02:31,573
A friend would help you moving your sofa, and a lover would want to have sex with you on the sofa.

26
00:02:32,533 --> 00:02:38,925
Now, as the intensity of relationships changes over time, earthlings need to synchronize the perceived relationship level.

27
00:02:39,206 --> 00:02:40,702
That's where greetings come in.

28
00:02:41,023 --> 00:02:43,093
Each level has its own greeting rituals.

29
00:02:43,729 --> 00:02:51,091
Acquaintances use formal greetings, friends more informal greetings, and lovers get all touchy and kissy and give each other cute animal names.

30
00:02:51,757 --> 00:02:57,097
Also, greetings serve to communicate and synchronize other information, like differences in social status.

31
00:02:59,317 --> 00:03:04,617
Tips for tourists: When you make first contact, you can use the formula "I come in peace".

32
00:03:05,077 --> 00:03:08,347
Anyway, earthlings will expect you to say something outlandish.

33
00:03:08,715 --> 00:03:16,662
However, the formula "Bring me to your leader" won't be very helpful, as most earthlings' social rank is too low to know their country's leader personally.

34
00:03:16,964 --> 00:03:19,677
The best you can expect is a meeting with some local official.

35
00:03:20,771 --> 00:03:25,798
To understand words like "Please" or "Thank you", we need to understand another mechanism: Gratitude.

36
00:03:26,365 --> 00:03:32,480
When trading goods or services with strangers, Earthlings use money - a virtual trade good I will explain in another episode.

37
00:03:32,843 --> 00:03:40,663
But for trade with friends and family they use mostly another virtual currency called gratitude, which is - unlike money - always bound to one relationship.

38
00:03:42,123 --> 00:03:45,585
Take, for example, an earthling A who needs help to move his sofa.

39
00:03:45,909 --> 00:03:51,061
He approaches B who greets him with "Hello, my friend", which indicates to A that they are still friends.

40
00:03:51,584 --> 00:03:55,904
So, A can offer B an exchange: Gratitude against help with the sofa.

41
00:03:56,324 --> 00:04:03,104
To initiate the exchange, A uses the ritual word "Please", which indicates a trade offer: Favour against gratitude.

42
00:04:03,104 --> 00:04:12,583
If B isn't interested in the trade, A may even increase the amount of offered gratitude, by changing the formula to something like "Pretty please" or "please please please with a cherry on top".

43
00:04:12,864 --> 00:04:16,544
It's basically a kind of bargaining, except that the currency is gratitude.

44
00:04:17,284 --> 00:04:21,014
Now, let's say B accepts the trade offer and helps him with his sofa.

45
00:04:21,258 --> 00:04:24,358
As agreed upon, A gives B gratitude in exchange.

46
00:04:24,778 --> 00:04:27,231
He does so with the ritual formula "Thank you".

47
00:04:27,231 --> 00:04:30,842
B receives the gratitude with another ritual formula, "You're welcome".

48
00:04:31,084 --> 00:04:34,842
The exact formulae may vary, in order to indicate the amount of gratitude.

49
00:04:35,345 --> 00:04:39,685
This is very important, to keep the perceived gratitude scores synchronized.

50
00:04:40,105 --> 00:04:43,144
B can then use the gratitude whenever he wants a favour from A.

51
00:04:43,716 --> 00:04:50,663
If A wants some gratitude back but B doesn't make a trade offer, he can do him a favour, or he can give him an object in exchange for gratitude.

52
00:04:51,307 --> 00:04:52,776
This is called a gift.

53
00:04:52,776 --> 00:04:58,136
Friends often exchange gifts, this improves the friendship and allows balancing the gratitude level.

54
00:04:58,558 --> 00:05:05,695
The strange thing about gratitude is that it is not exactly quantifiable or measurable - most earthlings don't even realize that it's a virtual trade good.

55
00:05:05,996 --> 00:05:10,220
But maintaining the gratitude flow is crucial for the functioning of any personal relationship.

56
00:05:10,846 --> 00:05:15,357
Actually, a relationship where one part gives and gives without receiving is called "exploitation".

57
00:05:16,259 --> 00:05:18,139
Or sometimes "friend zone".

58
00:05:21,219 --> 00:05:28,912
Scientific advice: If you have a time machine, you can make the following experiment: Abduct an earthling, bring him back in time and make him meet himself.

59
00:05:29,254 --> 00:05:35,743
The goal is to create a conflict situation - say, finding some delicious food after several days without eating.

60
00:05:36,164 --> 00:05:43,264
Many earthlings will come to the conclusion that social rules don't apply to their own time clones, which can lead to very interesting situations.

61
00:05:54,284 --> 00:05:59,524
Now, what happens if B does something bad to A, like ruining his sofa by accident?

62
00:05:59,884 --> 00:06:10,800
There is another mechanism for this kind of situation: In this case, B is considered at fault, which has three consequences: First, B is punished by the feeling of guilt, caused by his own niggler in his head.

63
00:06:11,281 --> 00:06:16,597
Second, A resents B for a while, which is just a ritual to communicate fault and maintain guilt.

64
00:06:17,083 --> 00:06:22,795
Third, to end this situation, B has to use another ritual formula, "Please excuse me" or "Sorry".

65
00:06:22,795 --> 00:06:29,575
As we have learned before, the word "please" opens a gratitude trade - in this case, gratitude against purging the fault.

66
00:06:29,955 --> 00:06:39,035
If A accepts the trade, he gets the gratitude and stops resenting B, and everything is as before - except for the ruined sofa and the modified gratitude scores.

67
00:06:40,915 --> 00:06:44,875
Strategic advice: "Sorry" is a very powerful word.

68
00:06:45,195 --> 00:06:50,890
When you attack earth, you might destroy one big earthling city, and then transmit the message "We are so sorry".

69
00:06:51,274 --> 00:06:56,690
If you banter something about technical issues or cultural misunderstandings, odds are that earthlings will believe you.

70
00:06:57,237 --> 00:06:59,512
However, this trick won't work a second time.

71
00:07:07,735 --> 00:07:15,462
Greetings, thanks and excuses are rituals - symbolic actions which don't actually do anything, but have an important place in earthling society.

72
00:07:15,784 --> 00:07:17,940
We will talk about rituals in another episode.

73
00:07:18,428 --> 00:07:22,474
By the way, ritualistic behaviour is common amongst mobile lifeforms on earth.

74
00:07:22,737 --> 00:07:24,517
Except for the microbes.

75
00:07:27,397 --> 00:07:30,577
Not everything can be explained with gratitude points, though.

76
00:07:31,037 --> 00:07:38,471
In large earthling settlements, you often see individuals called beggars who ask complete strangers for money, using the ritual word "please".

77
00:07:39,016 --> 00:07:48,404
Some alien scientists describe those beggars as "gratitude traders", but that doesn't make sense: Why should an earthling buy the gratitude of a stranger who will never ever help him with his sofa?

78
00:07:48,606 --> 00:07:53,026
And why should someone accumulate gratitude debts he can never expect to be able to pay back?

79
00:07:53,646 --> 00:07:56,864
I think there is another social mechanism behind this: Karma.

80
00:07:57,266 --> 00:08:04,551
Most earthlings have the more or less vague feeling that there is some kind of invisible point system, that you get points by being good and lose points by being bad.

81
00:08:04,954 --> 00:08:07,091
Some earthlings call these points "Karma".

82
00:08:07,605 --> 00:08:13,729
Some believe that karma is rewarded in the afterlife, in the next reincarnation, in this life, or not at all.

83
00:08:13,729 --> 00:08:20,184
But anyway, getting Karma causes a pleasant feeling of superiority, and makes the niggler in the head shut up for a while.

84
00:08:20,629 --> 00:08:30,023
If you consider Karma as a virtual exchange good, like money or gratitude, things become a lot clearer: Beggars are actual karma producers who sell their product on the street.

85
00:08:30,483 --> 00:08:36,207
Karma becomes kind of a luxury good: It's not vital, but it makes you feel good and helps to display your wealth.

86
00:08:36,207 --> 00:08:39,407
Not everybody buys karma directly from the producer, though.

87
00:08:39,927 --> 00:08:48,651
Many cities have intermediaries like soup kitchen where karma producers - poor earthlings - can sell their karma against food, and rich earthlings can buy karma against money.

88
00:08:49,134 --> 00:08:51,863
And that's not all: Karma is big business!

89
00:08:52,164 --> 00:09:01,909
Whenever earthlings in a region are struck by a natural catastrophe, international karma traders show up within hours, provide help, harvest karma and sell it to people in rich countries.

90
00:09:01,909 --> 00:09:06,909
This is called "humanitarian aid", but it is nothing else than world-wide karma trade.

91
00:09:07,149 --> 00:09:13,204
Roughly speaking, poor countries are in general karma exporters, whereas rich countries are generally karma importers.

92
00:09:13,729 --> 00:09:16,229
Karma comes, so to say, in different flavours.

93
00:09:17,189 --> 00:09:28,429
Besides humanitarian karma there exist, for example, animal protection karma, ecology karma, religious karma, patriotic karma, pacifist karma, and karma for buying from a specific company.

94
00:09:28,429 --> 00:09:35,691
A personal word to my earthling viewers: If you want some alien-flavoured karma, click on Like, subscribe and tell all your friends.

95
00:09:36,096 --> 00:09:43,864
And if you draw the attention of a popular blogger, Youtuber, rockstar, or whoever might bring me new traffic, I may also throw in some personal gratitude.

96
00:09:44,328 --> 00:09:50,693
In case you wonder why you would want the gratitude of a 3 feet high alien, I only say one word: Teleportation!

97
00:09:52,854 --> 00:10:02,431
In the next episode, I will explain something that most aliens find very puzzling: Funny things, humour, and the strange noises earthlings make when they find something funny.

98
00:10:02,973 --> 00:10:05,581
Thanks for watching, and don't forget to be alien!

